  displays them, there is no inbox and no badge. That's deliberate — building an event [3mlog[0m now would be speculative, but
  building the [3mfunnel[0m now is what stops sub-project 2 from having to hunt down every mutation site and retrofit an emit
  call. Sub-project 2 subscribes to a stream that already exists and already describes changes in the right vocabulary.

  That is the entire notification-readiness cost: one shape, one funnel, no features.

  [1mUI.[0m Into the existing empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m: an add-task input with an optional due date, and a single list showing incomplete
  tasks first with completed ones below. Per task — a checkbox, the title (click to edit inline), a due-date display,
  and a delete button. Overdue and due-today get visual emphasis, which is the honest v1 answer to "notice what needs
  attention" and which also tells us whether real notifications are even wanted before we build them. Full re-render on
  change; the list is small enough that diffing would be premature.

  [1mError handling.[0m Three failure modes, all of them about not losing your data silently:
  - [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m unavailable (private browsing, disabled) → run in-memory, show a persistent banner saying changes won't
    be saved.
  - Write fails or quota exceeded → keep the in-memory state, surface the failure, don't pretend it saved.
  - Stored JSON is corrupt or has an unknown [38;5;153mschemaVersion[39m → refuse to clobber it. Load empty, warn, leave the raw value
    untouched so it's recoverable.

  [1mTesting.[0m Vitest, ESLint + Prettier, per your tooling choices. Unit tests cover [38;5;153mmodel/[39m (pure, exhaustive) and [38;5;153mstore/[39m +
  [38;5;153mpersistence/[39m with a fake storage object injected rather than a real [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. UI stays thin enough that the
  untested surface is only DOM plumbing — no e2e, since you skipped it. TDD: tests first, per the normal workflow.

  [1mNot in v1[0m, explicitly: notification delivery of any kind, reminders, an event log, priorities, tags, notes, search,
  sync, accounts.

